/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (1.4.1 Emotional Support \(Psychological/Personal\))
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText <>
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Comfort foods are often staples in an individual's childhood that can be provided as a reward or have positive associations with consumption. Ice cream effectively evokes nostalgic emotions from most people's childhood, releasing both dopamine and serotonin when eaten \(Vandergriendt 2020\). These neurotransmitters provide psychological support and ultimately influence consumers to purchase ice cream. Furthermore, an individual's perception of flavour changes depending on the emotion felt, where sourness is tasted during challenging times \(Dando & Noel 2015\). The high content of sugar and fat within ice cream bypasses this limitation, ultimately serving as a beneficial decision regardless of the situation.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
